<p>Russia launched its heaviest two-day drone attack of the war against Ukraine, Ukrainian officials said. President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Moscow had fired more than 1,560 drones since Wednesday, including more than 670 attack drones and 56 missiles overnight into Thursday. Kyiv bore the brunt of the overnight barrage, with a residential block in the Darnytskyi district partially collapsing; later casualty reports said at least eight people were killed and more than 40 injured in the capital, with a child among the dead. The attacks followed the end on Monday of a three-day U.S.-brokered ceasefire and came after a daytime Russian drone barrage on Wednesday that Ukrainian officials said killed at least six people across about 20 regions. Ukrainian officials said civilian infrastructure, homes, railways, ports and energy facilities were hit; Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha said the timing, during President Trump’s visit to China, showed Moscow intended to keep fighting despite diplomatic efforts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/MRNnh8QwBvwlSlgZn0jqIv9BsLP0plwKsyL4JHOk5YDyFct1pSVlPO2tXL0kpHENjfJ9XatWYIchARtCgm7c6y8KdxFnkMzux2NtoSsxCJkzu2oBt0TYp3cTRDCRYTuwAXWCFbFu8sEZTWFmgVtM9eJO3gqCw_2LP9KlQu0dFlFpQMVy7NQVXLbE41qKmV9Bw0OG5QzW48j1Rc9DnA' alt='A residential building hit by Russian strikes in Kyiv, Ukraine, on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Wider damage: Ukrainian officials reported damage at more than 180 sites nationwide, including more than 50 residential buildings, while Kharkiv and the Odesa region also reported injuries.</li><li>Power outages: Ukraine’s Energy Ministry said attacks on energy infrastructure left some consumers in Kyiv without electricity and disrupted power in nearly half the country, including Volyn, Dnipropetrovsk, Donetsk, Zaporizhzhia, Kyiv, Lviv, Poltava, Sumy, Kharkiv, Chernihiv and Khmelnytskyi regions.</li></ul><blockquote>There should be no illusions or wishful thinking: only pressure on Moscow can force him to stop 